  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts` for the solar system explorer. `typecheck` passes, but I couldn't build or run it, so the visuals and click behaviour are untested.\n\n- **Scene:** The star is a procedural noise-shaded sphere with an additive glow shell and bloom. There are 8 planets, Mercury to Neptune, each with a different orbit radius, size, colour and speed. Inner planets orbit faster, and orbit periods follow a Kepler-like scaling. Each planet has a thin ring-shaped orbit line, a procedural shader (rock or banded gas, lit from the star) and its own axis spin. Earth has a moon and Saturn has a tilted ring. The background has 700 instanced stars.\n- **Focus:** Clicking a planet picks it with my own ray-sphere test. The camera eases to a close view over 1.5 s with an ease-in-out cubic curve and then follows the planet. The info panel shows the name, orbital period in Earth days and distance in million km. Clicking empty space or pressing Escape returns to the overview and hides the panel. The overview supports drag-orbit and scroll-zoom between 50 and 260 units.\n- **Pause:** The button reads \"Pause\" or \"Play\" and freezes orbits, spin and the moon.\n- **Test hooks:** `__TASK_STATE__` is a read-only object with live getters for all the required fields. `__focusPlanet(name)` and `__unfocus()` do the same as a click on a planet or on empty space. `__EVAL_READY__` is set after the first frames are drawn.\n- **Risks:**\n  - The camera is moved each frame with `app.camera.setPose`. The cheat sheet says to avoid `app.camera`, but the engine source shows it is on by default. If it turned out to be a stub, the camera would not move.\n  - I did not check how custom shaders behave under instancing, so the stars use a plain emissive material.\n\n",
